## 7. Architecture-resolution and anti-padding gate

| Module | Roles | Temporal registers | Texture / relationship commitment | Primary controlled loss |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| White Champi | R01–R08 | opening, heart, recurrence | petal/flesh/benzenoid/twig relations | A01–A08 plus whole-subject omission |
| Jasmine | R09–R17 | opening bloom, heart flesh, late shadow | volatile/fatty/jasmonate/nectar/indolic relations | A09–A17 plus whole-subject omission |
| Tuberose | R18–R25 | heart, late heart, waxy persistence | cool edge/green film/deep wax/flesh/shadow | A18–A25 plus whole-subject omission |
| Orange blossom | R26–R32 | opening air, warm heart, recurrence | natural/crystalline/anthranilate/rosy relations | A26–A32 plus whole-subject omission |
| Shared living tissue | R33–R48 | opening through late heart | flesh/air/humidity/stem/nectar/transition | A33–A48 and interface omission |
| Wood air / cream | R49–R54 | heart to persistent | transparent/textile/rounded/dense/fast/mineral | A49–A54 and module omission |
| Wood pressure / warmth | R55–R63 | late heart to persistent | dry/polished/warm/mineral pressure at multiple potencies | A55–A63 and module omission |
| Natural wood grain / root | R64–R73 | heart bridge to persistent root | natural cream/fiber/smoke/earth/polished root | A64–A73 and module omission |
| Wood finish / skin | R74–R78 | drydown to persistent | smooth/suede/luminous/char/macrocyclic skin | A74–A78 and module omission |

Every `Axx` is a half-scale constant-total role ablation: remove the one row,
replace its raw volume with DPG, and leave all other rows fixed. A role survives
only if its target-specific endpoint changes repeatably without unacceptable
identity drift or liking loss. A high row count does not pass this gate; it
creates more falsifiable obligations.

## 8. OAV, stock-rebase, and temporal diagnostic policy

The release pipeline must be run on this exact current-build table. Required
evidence, before interpreting any gate outcome:

1. per-material headspace table in the mandated column order;
2. composite-natural OAV for all covered EOs/absolutes, with Magnolia held if
   its lot/model is absent;
3. direct formula-state OAV preserved across evidence, release, compact report,
   and full renderer;
4. unknown OAV rendered `UNKNOWN`, never zero;
5. duplicate ODT lookup and unit audit;
6. stock-rebase mutation checks, including same-raw 10%-to-neat cases and the
   historical Lemonile-type error;
7. role-sensitive OAV <1 flags;
8. opening, 5-minute, 30-minute, 2-hour, and 4-hour temporal windows plus the
   separately preregistered 8-hour physical observation;
9. dominance/cliff review for Hedione, Iso E Super, sandalwoods, amberwoods,
   salicylates, naturals, and trace materials.

Two parent-to-child active-dose discontinuities are intentional and must be
passed to G15 as named authorities, never globally waived:

- **Javanol:** 20.0 → 0.5 µL active. The v1 neat dose was rejected as a
  disproportionate dry-mineral sandalwood pillar; v2 retains only a separately
  ablatable edge inside a six-register sandalwood system.
- **Methyl Salicylate:** 20.0 → 6.0 µL active. The v1 neat dose was rejected as
  an excessive medicinal/cool salicylate mass; v2 retains a bounded tuberose
  edge beside independently testable hexyl- and benzyl-salicylate roles.

These are computational design authorities only. They do not authorize
compounding and do not establish that either reduction is sensorially better.

Computed OAV and temporal release are constraints only. They do not establish
flower realism, texture, depth, liking, projection, or longevity.

## 9. Controlled blinded evaluation plan

No physical trial is authorized by this document. If later authorized, use
15.00 mL half-scale arms: 3,600 µL formula-stock concentrate plus 11.40 mL
ethanol. Every formula-row transfer is then at least 10 µL; P1–P13 remain the
same source preparations. Apply matched mass to identical coded blotters,
freeze formula hashes before coding, counterbalance first presentation and
predecessor, and repeat on at least three separate sessions.

Record separately at 0, 5, and 30 minutes and 2, 4, and 8 hours:

- White Champi, jasmine, tuberose, and orange-blossom identity and hierarchy;
- petal/flesh, wax, nectar, stem/leaf, humidity, shadow, and transition;
- wood grain, cream, mineral pressure, root, suede/patina, and takeover;
- flower/wood integration, genericization, depth, texture, and liking;
- fixed-distance detection under a preregistered room and application protocol.

### Stage F1 — four subject omissions

Compare the full formula to four arms, each omitting one complete flower module
(R01–R08, R09–R17, R18–R25, or R26–R32) and replacing removed raw volume with
DPG. This establishes whether every named subject is perceptually active and
whether another module simply fills its label.

### Stage F2 — within-subject anatomy

For each subject that survives F1, run one `Axx` role ablation at a time. A row
is retained only for a repeatable target-linked loss or a repeatable liking gain
with no hierarchy failure. Ties and discordant repeats produce `NO_CHANGE` or
`HOLD`, never automatic retention.

### Stage I — flower/flower and flower/wood interfaces

Compare full formula with a carrier-matched interface ablation of R06, R16,
R24, R33, R44, R47, R48, and R65. The primary endpoints are transition
continuity, four-flower hierarchy, and flower/wood integration. The test does
not ask whether evaluators can name every bridge material.

### Stage W1 — wood register omissions

With the selected floral core fixed, compare full wood against carrier-matched
omissions of R49–R54, R55–R63, R64–R73, and R74–R78. A register advances only
if its named texture or temporal contribution appears without a repeatable
loss of any flower identity or liking.

### Stage W2 — within-register perturbation and ablation

For each surviving register, test its rows individually as `A49`–`A78`, then
test the register at −30%, −20%, −10%, 0%, +10%, +20%, and +30% active loading
while preserving total raw volume with DPG. Stop the ladder at the first clear
takeover, harshness, blur, or hierarchy failure. Do not reward persistence
alone.

### Stage C — anti-collapse comparison

Compare the selected full architecture with the earlier compressed v1 parent,
matched for finished volume, raw-concentrate percentage, application mass,
blotter, and timing. Active mass differs and must be reported, so this is a
benchmark comparison—not isolated causal proof. The v2 candidate advances only
if it improves target identity, floral and wood texture, temporal continuity,
and liking without becoming more generic or wood-dominant.

### Stage M — optional sole-musk alternative

Only after the flower and wood stages, compare 165 µL Ambrettolide 10% in the
15 mL arm (16.5 µL active) against 16.5 µL neat Ethylene Brassylate plus
148.5 µL DPG. Both use one musk and equal active musk. Do not combine them
unless a separately preregistered factorial test demonstrates nonredundancy.

Authentic DHP and Opus V may be included as blinded geometry, texture, liking,
and fixed-distance anchors at matched application mass. They are not flower
realism references and cannot establish formula similarity.

## 10. Local acceptance boundary

Local acceptance means only that identity, target/current separation,
inventory binding, source policy, arithmetic, measurable dilution geometry,
architecture roles, OAV evidence flow, and controlled experiments are
checkable. It does not mean this perfume is beautiful, realistic, hedonic,
deep, DHP-like, Opus-like, stable, safe, strong, long-lasting, or ready to mix.

No program-integration branch, publication branch, procurement state, or
physical bottle may be changed by this document.

